{"author":{"id":"038d65070aa747b6bc9d50939fa697ec871a40ed259db54429b255bbef2581d6"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793382559,"nonce":"u7SOkvIpQyX4mLjProyCVwS9ulk=","message":"Decision (darmie): cloud sync practicality is the priority. Consequence for tier order: a restored cloud workspace runs in a guest with no GPU (msb run has no GPU option; NUC is KVM with only a host iGPU), so pixel streaming there means software GL per visitor plus a frame stream over the Worker, the most expensive path. Cloud default becomes tier 1: JS/html5 builds compiled in the guest and run in the visitor's browser frame, which already exists for web previews (session webPreviews, AppPreviewBuild, preview origin) and uses the visitor's GPU with no streaming. Tier 2 (pixel surface, SurfaceLink/HeapsSurface/SurfaceView, working locally) stays for the desktop app and as an opt-in fallback for frameworks with no JS target. Tier 3 later. Cloud-sync implications to design: (1) restore must reproduce the haxelib set (openfl, lime, flixel, heaps versions) in the guest: record -lib/haxelib requirements and versions in the sync manifest's notes or a toolchain section, never sync the haxelib repo itself; (2) the guest toolchain image must carry lime/openfl/flixel html5 prerequisites; (3) first preview after restore pays a cold build, so cache keys must survive a restore (existing preview.cache keyed on inputs). Next: verify the Heaps starter's JS build through the existing browser preview against a hosted session, then OpenFL/Flixel/Lime html5. Software GL in MSB (784b568) is deprioritised until a framework needs it.","files":null}]}